{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005254","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Among the 8 retrieved candidates, only three are direct aortic-stenosis disease models by trait_reported/trait_efo (PGS005252, PGS005254), or are aortic-valve-function traits but still use an incident aortic stenosis endpoint in phenotyping_reported (PGS005255, PGS005256). The hypertrophic cardiomyopathy models are not direct matches.

Between the two explicit aortic stenosis scores, PGS005254 has strong endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported: incident aortic stenosis) with relatively clean/basic covariate adjustment (sex, age, age^2, PCs 1–5) and reports a per-SD hazard ratio with CI (HR=1.64, 1.50–1.78), providing direct evidence of PRS contribution. PGS005252 also matches the endpoint (incident aortic stenosis) and reports a high C-index (0.87) but its covariate set includes multiple cardiovascular comorbidities and risk factors (e.g., T2D, HTN, CAD, hyperlipidemia, BMI, smoking, renal failure), which the provided domain_knowledge flags as likely to inflate full-model discrimination and make it non-comparable for PRS ranking when incremental/PGS-only metrics are not shown. The valve-function trait scores (PGS005255/PGS005256) are less directly aligned by trait_reported even though their phenotyping_reported is incident aortic stenosis.

Main limitation: none of the aortic stenosis candidates report clearly PRS-only discrimination metrics (PGS-only AUROC/R² or incremental AUROC), so selection relies on endpoint fidelity, covariate comparability, and effect-size evidence; this keeps confidence below high."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS005254","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Among the 8 retrieved candidates, four (PGS000739/000778/004910/004911) are for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and are not phenotype-aligned to the target trait (aortic stenosis). The remaining four are aortic-stenosis–aligned by trait_efo, but only PGS005252 and PGS005254 are explicitly labeled and phenotyped as aortic stenosis (PGS005255/PGS005256 are labeled as aortic valve function measures even though they use an incident aortic stenosis endpoint, making endpoint intent less directly aligned to a generic aortic stenosis PRS).

Between the two clear aortic stenosis candidates, PGS005254 is preferred because it has endpoint fidelity (incident aortic stenosis), uses only basic demographic/PC covariates (age, age^2, sex, PCs 1–5), and reports an effect size with CI (HR=1.64, 95% CI 1.50–1.78) in a large validation sample (n=244,450). By contrast, PGS005252 reports a very high full-model C-index=0.87 but includes extensive cardiovascular comorbidity covariates (e.g., T2D, HTN, CAD, hyperlipidemia, renal failure, BMI, smoking), which domain guidance flags as likely to inflate/obscure PRS contribution; it also provides no PRS-only/incremental metric to isolate PRS performance.

Main limitation: neither leading aortic stenosis model provides explicit PRS-only AUROC/R² or incremental AUROC in the visible metadata, so the choice relies on endpoint + covariate comparability and effect-size evidence rather than directly comparable PRS-only discrimination metrics."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005220","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates for obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) are present (PGS003479, PGS003857, PGS003858, PGS005219, PGS005220), plus multiple broader “sleep apnea” ExPRSweb models. Per the provided domain policy, OSA-labeled models should be preferred over generic sleep-apnea labels when supported.

Among the OSA-labeled candidates, PGS005220 (UnadjustedBMI) has (i) clear phenotype alignment (“Obstructive sleep apnea” in phenotyping_reported), (ii) a disease-focused publication context (“Polygenic scores for obstructive sleep apnoea…”), (iii) multi-cohort development listed (FinnGen, MGBB, MVP) suggesting broader study support than the ExPRSweb framework models, and (iv) the strongest available performance signal in context (full_model AUROC=0.79 and OR=1.53 [1.39–1.68]) with a moderate validation sample size (n=21,975). Compared with PGS005219 (AdjustedBMI), PGS005220 better matches the generic target trait “obstructive sleep apnea” without the BMI-adjusted endpoint framing.

Main limitation: the reported AUROC appears to be a full-model metric and covariates include BMI (a major OSA risk factor), so the AUROC is not clearly PRS-only/comparable; several other OSA candidates (PGS003857/PGS003858) report similar or slightly lower full-model AUROC (0.77) but also include BMI in covariates, and some metadata are potentially inconsistent (e.g., PGS003479 has sparse/unclear phenotyping_reported and no AUC). Because PRS-only discrimination/incremental metrics are not provided, the evidence supports a preference but not a high-certainty, unconflicted best-in-class choice."}
